# ── Schema snapshots must round-trip byte-for-byte ─────────────────────────
#
# This repo has core.autocrlf=true, so without this rule git rewrites line
# endings on checkout. schema-snapshot.mjs writes LF (it undoes the CRLF that
# psql and pg_dump print on Windows), and some function bodies hold a real
# CR that is part of the database's own text. If git touched either, a fresh
# dump would never match the committed one, and `npm run schema:check` would
# report drift on a perfectly clean database — forever. A check that always
# cries wolf is a check somebody switches off.
#
# Proven, not assumed: committing the dump without this rule changed its hash
# from 602b8add to bd24ad67; with it, the hash survives the round trip intact.
#
# Deliberately scoped to the snapshot directory. A blanket `*.sql` rule would
# renormalise all 143 existing migration files and produce a diff touching
# every one of them.
mobile/supabase/schema/** -text

# ── Shell scripts keep LF everywhere ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The same core.autocrlf=true checked mobile/e2e/run-flows.sh out with CRLF on
# Windows, and bash reads the CR as part of every command ("$'\r': command not
# found"). CI checks out on Linux, so a script could pass there and break here
# — and the E2E runner's rehearsal (e2e/__tests__/runFlows.rehearsal.test.ts)
# runs it on both. Every *.sh was already stored with LF, so this rule changes
# no committed byte; it only stops git from adding the CRs on checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf
